y, in a conversation, I should address all pending requests or the most recent one based on context. The second query is about shipping status.\n\nI have already searched for shipping in the previous turn and found one result:\n- Sender: Acme Store\n- Subject: Your order A-42 has shipped\n- Date: Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\n- Mailbox: ana@harness.test\n\nThe first query (rebrand review time/calendar) has not been addressed yet. Since the user sent a second, unrelated message, I should first answer the shipping question clearly and then check if I can find the \"rebrand review details as they are likely related to the current context.\n\nHowever, if I treat the second message as the only active request, I will answer the shipping one. I will check my prior thought process. The prompt says \"Work out everything the request needs and search for all of it before you start answering, issuing those searches together rather than one per turn.\" When multiple users send messages, it''s a continuous conversation. I should have done the shipping search in anticipation.\"rebrand review\" in parallel.\n\nSince the user sent two messages, and I only performed the search for the shipping question, I should ideally do the \"rebrand review\" search now, which is Turn 1.\n\n\"rebrand review\" is very vague. I''ll search for \"rebrand review\" and \"rebrand\" in mail and calendar.\n\n**Plan for Turn 3 (Current turn):**\n1. Answer shipping question based on previous searches.\n2. Search for \"rebrand review\" in mail (Turn 1 question).\n3. Search for \"rebrand review\" in calendar (Turn 1 question). Search).\n\n**Search for \"rebrand review\":**\nMail: searchMail \"rebrand review\"\nCalendar: listEventsThe search results contain one specific instance of a shipped order:\n1.  **From**: Acme Store\n2.  **Subject**: Your order A-42 has shipped\n3.  **Date**: Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\n4.  **Mailbox**: ana@harness.test\n\nSince the query was \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\", this response directly answers the question using data found in the search. I will summarize this finding.The user is asking if any orders of theirs have shipped recently.\nI performed several searches in `searchMail` with keywords like \"shipped\", \"order\", \"tracking\", and \"shipment\".\n\nThe results show one relevant email from \"Acme Store\" with the subject \"Your order A-42 has shipped\".\nDate: Wednesday, September 23, 2026, 10:00:00 GMT+0000.\nMailbox: ana@harness.test.\n\nThis email is a clear match for \"did any order of mine ship recently?\".\n\nI should report this to the user. No further searches or actions are needed.The search for \"order\", \"shipped\", \"tracking\", and \"shipment\" returned several results.\n\nThe most relevant result is:\n- Subject: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\"\n- From: \"Acme Store\"\n- Date: \"Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)\"\n- Mailbox: \"ana@harness.test\"\n\nI will summarize this finding for the user.The user asks \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\".\nThe search results contain an email from \"Acme Store\" with the subject \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" on \"Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\".\n\nI should report this finding to the user.\nI will compile the result into a clear, scannable response.The user asked if any of their orders shipped recently.\nI searched mail for \"shipped\", \"order\", \"tracking\", \"shipment\" across all mailboxes.\nThe search results show one definitive email:\n- Subject: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\"\n- From: Acme Store\n- Date: Wednesday, September 23, 2020 10:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)\n- Mailbox: ana@harness.test\n\nThis occurred on Sept 23, which is recent relative to the query date (Oct 2026).\nI will report this finding.I have a new request: \"What time is the rebrand review and on which calendar?\" This is a calendar-related request. I need to search calendars.\n\nBefore searching, I should list calendars to know the available scopes, although the promptOne match: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" from Acme Store, dated Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00 UTC, in ana@harness.test. But \"recently\" — today is 2026-10-27, so Sep 23 is over a month ago. Hmm. Is that \"recently\"? Maybe there''s a newer shipment notification that didn''t match \"shipped\" exactly. Let me check the inbox recent emails via aggregateInbox to see if there''s a shipping notification more recently. The user''s turn is 2026-10-27; Sep 23 is ~5 weeks ago. \"Recently\" could mean within the last few days. Let me check aggregateInbox for recent emails and look for order/shipping notifications. Also maybe search \"delivered\", \"package\", \"courier\", \"parcel\", \"UPS\", \"FedEx\".\n\nLet me run aggregateInbox and a couple more searches.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user asked \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\".\nI searched mail for \"shipped\", \"order\", \"tracking\", \"shipment\".\nThe results show one definite match:\n1. Subject: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\"\n2. From: \"Acme Store\"\n3. Date: \"Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)\"\n4. Mailbox: \"ana@harness.test\"\n\nI should present this information to the user.The user asks two questions across two turns:\n1. \"What time is the rebrand review and on which calendar?\" (Turn 1)\n2. \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\" (Turn 2)\n\nThe current request is driven by the second user message. However, usually, in a conversation, I should address all pending requests or the most recent one based on context. The second query is about shipping status.\n\nI have already searched for shipping in the previous turn and found one result:\n- Sender: Acme Store\n- Subject: Your order A-42 has shipped\n- Date: Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\n- Mailbox: ana@harness.test\n\nThe first query (rebrand review time/calendar) has not been addressed yet. Since the user sent a second, unrelated message, I should first answer the shipping question clearly and then check if I can find the \"rebrand review details as they are likely related to the current context.\n\nHowever, if I treat the second message as the only active request, I will answer the shipping one. I will check my prior thought process. The prompt says \"Work out everything the request needs and search for all of it before you start answering, issuing those searches together rather than one per turn.\" When multiple users send messages, it''s a continuous conversation. I should have done the shipping search in anticipation.\"rebrand review\" in parallel.\n\nSince the user sent two messages, and I only performed the search for the shipping question, I should ideally do the \"rebrand review\" search now, which is Turn 1.\n\n\"rebrand review\" is very vague. I''ll search for \"rebrand review\" and \"rebrand\" in mail and calendar.\n\n**Plan for Turn 3 (Current turn):**\n1. Answer shipping question based on previous searches.\n2. Search for \"rebrand review\" in mail (Turn 1 question).\n3. Search for \"rebrand review\" in calendar (Turn 1 question). Search).\n\n**Search for \"rebrand review\":**\nMail: searchMail \"rebrand review\"\nCalendar: listEventsThe search results contain one specific instance of a shipped order:\n1.  **From**: Acme Store\n2.  **Subject**: Your order A-42 has shipped\n3.  **Date**: Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\n4.  **Mailbox**: ana@harness.test\n\nSince the query was \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\", this response directly answers the question using data found in the search. I will summarize this finding.The user is asking if any orders of theirs have shipped recently.\nI performed several searches in `searchMail` with keywords like \"shipped\", \"order\", \"tracking\", and \"shipment\".\n\nThe results show one relevant email from \"Acme Store\" with the subject \"Your order A-42 has shipped\".\nDate: Wednesday, September 23, 2026, 10:00:00 GMT+0000.\nMailbox: ana@harness.test.\n\nThis email is a clear match for \"did any order of mine ship recently?\".\n\nI should report this to the user. No further searches or actions are needed.The search for \"order\", \"shipped\", \"tracking\", and \"shipment\" returned several results.\n\nThe most relevant result is:\n- Subject: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\"\n- From: \"Acme Store\"\n- Date: \"Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)\"\n- Mailbox: \"ana@harness.test\"\n\nI will summarize this finding for the user.The user asks \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\".\nThe search results contain an email from \"Acme Store\" with the subject \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" on \"Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT+0000\".\n\nI should report this finding to the user.\nI will compile the result into a clear, scannable response.The user asked if any of their orders shipped recently.\nI searched mail for \"shipped\", \"order\", \"tracking\", \"shipment\" across all mailboxes.\nThe search results show one definitive email:\n- Subject: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\"\n- From: Acme Store\n- Date: Wednesday, September 23, 2020 10:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)\n- Mailbox: ana@harness.test\n\nThis occurred on Sept 23, which is recent relative to the query date (Oct 2026).\nI will report this finding.I have a new request: \"What time is the rebrand review and on which calendar?\" This is a calendar-related request. I need to search calendars.\n\nBefore searching, I should list calendars to know the available scopes, although the promptOne match: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" from Acme Store, dated Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00 UTC, in ana@harness.test. But \"recently\" — today is 2026-10-27, so Sep 23 is over a month ago. Hmm. Is that \"recently\"? Maybe there''s a newer shipment notification that didn''t match \"shipped\" exactly. Let me check the inbox recent emails via aggregateInbox to see if there''s a shipping notification more recently. The user''s turn is 2026-10-27; Sep 23 is ~5 weeks ago. \"Recently\" could