ails in the specified mailbox (`ana@harness.test`).\n\nSince the primary mailbox is the only one involved, and `searchMail` (which searches the whole mailbox by default, restricted here for focus) returned 0, I can conclude the count is 0.\n\nIf `searchMail` had returned 0, I wouldn''t need to waste turns paging through the entire mailbox.\n\nFinal check: The user asks how many unread emails are in their inbox. The response is 0.\n\nI will state the count.The `searchMail` call for \"is:unread in:inbox\" returned a count of 0 and no matching emails. This strongly suggests that there are no unread emails in that specific mailbox (`ana@harness.test`).\n\nTo be absolutely certain, I would theoretically need to check all other mailboxes (if any were visible/loaded), but since the prompt only focuses on the user''s inbox and the search result specifically targets that mailbox and returned 0, I can confidently report 0.\n\nFinal answer formulation: 0 unread emails.That query likely treated as text — count 0 means no emails containing \"is:unread in:inbox\" text. Not conclusive.\n\nI should page the inbox and count unread. Let''s page. The inbox might have many edge emails (thousands?). Hmm, this could be expensive. Let''s page a few times and count. If we find unread ones, count grows. Actually the response''s \"count\" is total? No, \"count\":50 is number returned in this page. The listEmails response has nextPageToken \"235\". That''s a token, not an offset necessarily.\n\nLet''s page. I''ll fetch page 2 and 3 in parallel? Pagination requires sequential tokens. But maybe the token \"235\" is an offset and pages are deterministic; still need the returned token each time.\n\nLet''s page sequentially. Each call returns nextPageToken. I''ll count unread per page.\n\nLet me fetch page 2."}],"createdAt":1793064507346},{"type":"tool-invocation","toolInvocation":{"state":"result","result":{"mailbox":"ana@harness.test","count":50,"nextPageToken":"185","emails":[{"id":"WyJJTkJPWCI