INSERT INTO "mastra_messages" ("id","thread_id","content","role","type","createdAt","resourceId") VALUES ('401e512e-7c32-44cc-8f7e-3368fc7289ed','b3d64685-da74-4e2b-af2a-7b3b775f4d8b','{"format":2,"parts":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"The user wants to find emails that resemble newsletters or digests.\nI need to search the email content/subject for keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"promotion\", etc., across all connected mailboxes.\n\nI will use the `searchMail` tool for this.\nThe keywords should cover common patterns for newsletters and digests.\nThe scope is all mailboxes, so I won''t specify `mailboxId`.The user wants to find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\nSince this is a search query across the workspace, I need to use `searchMail`.\nI should look for common keywords associated with newsletters, digests, marketing, or subscription emails.\nI will search across all mailboxes by default asMailboxId=null.\n\nPlan:\n1. Call `searchMail` with keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"summary\", \"daily\", \"weekly\", \"monthly\" across all mailboxes.\n2. Report the results, including the source mailbox for each email.The user wants to find emails that \"look like newsletters or digests\".\nLooking at the available tools and capabilities:\n1.  **searchMail**: Can search every mailbox.\n2.  **Keyword/Content matching**: \"newsletter\" or \"digest\" are good keywords.\n3.  **Date/Recency**: \"recently\" was used in the previous turn, but the current turn doesn''t specify time, so I should probably stick to searching across all mailboxes for these keywords, or if I want to prioritize \"recent\" content, I can filter by date, but since the prompt is generic now, I''ll use keywords first.\n\nI will call `searchMail` with keywords related to newsletters/digests. Since the user didn''t specify a mailbox, `searchMail` will search across all of them.\n\nSearch query: \"newsletter\" OR \"digest\"\nI should also include common sender patterns or \"unsubscribe, as those often indicate a bulk/marketing email, but starting with \"newsletter\" and \"digest\" is cleaner for a first pass.\n\nTool usage: `searchMail` (default mailbox)\nKeywords: \"newsletter\" OR \"digest\"\n\nLet''s execute the search.The user wants me to find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\nThis requires a search across all available mailboxes.\nI will use the `searchMail` tool with relevant keywords and search across all mailboxes.\n\nRelevant keywords: \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"summary\", \"daily\", \"weekly\", \"report\", \"digest\", \"blast\", etc.\n\nI need to check the available tool `searchMail`.\nParameters: `searchQuery` (string), `mailboxId` (string, optional). Since I need to search everywhere, I will omit `mailboxId`.\nThe prompt says `searchMail (every mailbox by default, one when you pass mailboxId)`.\n\nI will formulate a search query combining these concepts.\n\nTool call: `searchMail` with keywords.The user wants me to find emails that appear to be newsletters or digests.\nThe previous turn retrieved the 50 most recent emails from the inbox of `ana@harness.test`. These emails were all from \"Edge Sender\" and had the subject \"Re: Long thread\". These are not likely to be newsletters/digests.\n\nTo fulfill this request, I need to search all connected mailboxes for patterns typical of newsletters or digests, such as keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", or common branding/sending patterns.\n\nTool: `searchMail`\nKeywords: \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"report\"\nScope: All mailboxes (since no specific mailbox was mentioned).\n\nI will use `searchMail` with keywords related to newsletters/digests.The user wants to find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\nI need to search across all mailboxes for emails with typical newsletter/digest characteristics (e.g., subject lines like \"Your weekly digest\", \"Weekly update\", or from known marketing/news sources).\n\nSince the previous turn returned emails from \"Edge Sender\" in \"ana@harness.test\", I should start by searching with keywords and filtering for patterns typical of newsletters.\n\nKeywords to use for `searchMail`: \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"weekly update\", \"daily summary\", \"bi-weekly\", etc.\nI will search across all mailboxes by default.\n\nPlan:\n1. Call `searchMail` with keywords related to newsletters and digests across all mailboxes.\n2. Present the findings to the user, noting the source mailbox for each.The user wants me to find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\nThis is a general request that suggests searching the inbox for specific types of content.\nSince I have access to `searchMail`, I should use that tool.\nI will search across all available mailboxes (as per instructions when no specific mailbox is referenced) for keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", or common patterns associated with these types of emails.\nI don''t need to load new tools as `searchMail` and related tools are always available.\n\nPlan:\n1. Call `searchMail` with keywords \"newsletter\", \"digest\".\n2. Report the findings.\nThe user did not specify a mailbox, so I will search across all of them.\nI see one mailbox `ana@harness.test` from the previous turn, but the instruction states to search across all by default. I will search all available mailboxes.Wait — the previous turn seems to have gone off the rails. The conversation history shows me paging the inbox looking for unread emails, which was not the user''s question. The user''s actual question was \"Did any order of mine ship recently?\" I found one email: \"Your order A-42 has shipped\" from Acme Store, dated Wed Sep 23 2026 10:00:00 GMT. That''s about a month before the turn sent-at (Oct 27). Hmm, \"recently\" — that''s ~34 days ago. I never answered the first question! The assistant messages in history... Actually looking at the transcript: my first response was tool calls to searchMail (four searches), and the results came back. Then there was apparently a turn where I called listEmails with pageToken 185 — that looks like a mistakenly-generated turn about \"unread emails\" (not the user''s request). The user then sent a new message at 01:28.\n\nSo I should now address BOTH: answer the shipment question properly (it was left unanswered) and do the new request: find newsletters/digests.\n\nActually the current user message is \"Find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\" I should do that primarily, but I should also answer the shipping question since it was never answered. Better: do the newsletter search now and include a brief note on the shipment finding.\n\nLet me search for newsletters/digests. Keywords: \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"weekly\", \"unsubscribe\", \"this week at the shop\", \"roundup\", \"briefing\", \"bulletin\".\n\nLet me search across all mailboxes.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The user wants to find emails that resemble newsletters or digests.\nI need to search the email content/subject for keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"promotion\", etc., across all connected mailboxes.\n\nI will use the `searchMail` tool for this.\nThe keywords should cover common patterns for newsletters and digests.\nThe scope is all mailboxes, so I won''t specify `mailboxId`.The user wants to find emails that look like newsletters or digests.\nSince this is a search query across the workspace, I need to use `searchMail`.\nI should look for common keywords associated with newsletters, digests, marketing, or subscription emails.\nI will search across all mailboxes by default asMailboxId=null.\n\nPlan:\n1. Call `searchMail` with keywords like \"newsletter\", \"digest\", \"update\", \"summary\", \"daily\", \"weekly\", \"monthly\" across all mailboxes.\n2. Report the results, including the source mailbox for each email.The user wants to find emails that \"look like newsletters or digests\".\nLooking at the availab