INSERT INTO "mastra_messages" ("id","thread_id","content","role","type","createdAt","resourceId") VALUES ('de0e59c7-8abb-4f0d-a77c-268b73e671cb','b3d64685-da74-4e2b-af2a-7b3b775f4d8b','{"format":2,"parts":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"The user wants a \"prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above.\"\n\"Everything above\" refers to:\n1. The user''s workspace context (Ana Silva, ana@harness.test).\n2. The available tools and capabilities (Mail, Calendar, Drive, Workflows).\n3. The previous interaction: checking for unread emails (but no results were returned, only a response saying I needed to check).\n\nThe user is asking for a summary or a list of tasks. Since I am a Workspace AI, a \"to-do list\" typically means action items derived from emails, calendar events, or document action points.\n\nI need to:\n1.  Check for unread emails in the inbox. (This is the most direct \"to-do\" source).\n2.  Check for calendar events this week to see meetings/appointments.\n3.  Search drives/mail for urgent items or specific action requests (though the prompt doesn''t specify *why* they need a to-do list).\n\n**Action Plan:**\n1.  Search for tools related to listing emails/inboxes, calendar events, and tasks/action items.\n2.  List all mailboxes/drives to know where to look.\n3.  Check for unread emails.\n4.  Check for calendar events for this week.\n5.  Consolidate findings into a prioritized to-do list.\n\nSince the previous turn was about *checking* for unread mail and I haven''t executed that check yet, I must execute the searches now. I need to check the inbox contents.\n\n**Step 1: Search for relevant tools (already know some, but good to check for \"to-do\" or \"priority\" specific ones).**\n`listEmails` from the previous turn should work for checking the inbox.\n\n**Step 2: Get workspace information.**\n`listMailboxes` (to know all inboxes).\n`listCalendars` (to know all calendars).\n`listDrives` (to know all drives).\n\n**Step 3: Define the time frame.**\nThe user sent the request at `2026-10-27T01:32:26.836Z`. \"This week\" usually refers to the current calendar week (Monday to Sunday). Since today is Friday (Oct 27th), \"this week\" usually means from Oct 26th to Nov 1st, or starting from the beginning of the current week (Oct 26th in this case). I will check for events starting from the beginning of the week.\n\nI will execute the necessary searches.The user is asking for a prioritized to-do list for this week based on \"everything above.\"\n\"Everything above\" refers to the initial context, which is an empty conversation since the AI has just started and the user''s previous turn was a question about unread emails, which resulted in metadata/tool discovery.\n\nSince I have no context about specific unread emails, calendar events, or drive files from the conversation history *beyond* the tool discovery/member listing, I cannot generate a meaningful to-do list.\n\nThe available tools are for mail, calendar, and drive management.\n\n1.  **Check for unread emails:** I know `listEmails` is available, but I need a `mailboxId` to check the inbox.\n2.  **Check for calendar events:** I don''t see any calendar listings yet. I should probably list them.\n3.  **Check for drive files:** No drives have been listed or referenced. I should list them.\n\nSince the request is for a \"prioritized to-do list for this week,\" this implies I should look at:\n1.  Inbox (for urgent emails)\n2.  Calendar (for meetings/deadlines)\n3.  Drive (for pending tasks related to documents)\n\nI need to perform listing actions first to gather the necessary data to answer the request, as I have no contextual information.\n\nI will search for the required listing tools:\n1.  `listMailboxes` (to find my inbox)\n2.  `listCalendars` (to find my calendar)\n3.  `listDrives` (to find any connected drives)\n\nI will call `listMailboxes`, `listCalendars`, and `listDrives` simultaneously, and then search for a tool that can summarize or analyze tasks/to-do lists across these sources.\n\nFirst, let''s list the sources.The user wants a prioritized to-do list for \"this week\" based on \"everything above.\" Turn sent-at is 2026-10-27T01:32Z (Tuesday, Oct 27, 2026 — let me verify: Oct 27, 2026 is a Tuesday. Actually 2026-10-27... Jan 1 2026 is Thursday. Let me compute: 2026-10-27. Days: 2026 is not leap. Jan 1 = Thursday. Day of year for Oct 27: 31+28+31+30+31+30+31+31+30+27 = 300. 300 mod 7 = 6 (299 days after Jan 1... day 1 = Thursday, so day 300 = Thursday + 299 = 299 mod 7 = 5 → Tuesday). So Tuesday Oct 27, 2026. \"This week\" = Mon Oct 26 – Sun Nov 1, 2026.\n\nEverything above = the inbox emails I retrieved. But all I''ve seen so far: 100 emails from the inbox, all read, mostly \"Edge Sender\" test junk from Sep 10, plus a handful of real-looking ones (Shop Weekly, Harness Bank debit, Raj Mehta rebrand invitation for Thu Oct 8 2026 15:00 UTC — which is in the past relative to Oct 27! Wait, invitation says \"Thu Oct 8, 2026 15:00 UTC\" — Oct 8 2026 is a Thursday. That''s before Oct 27. So that event already passed).\n\nHmm. So \"everything above\" is thin: an inbox full of edge-case test emails. A prioritized to-do list would need more substance. I should gather actual signal: recent inbox across all mailboxes (aggregateInbox), unread emails (there appear to be none), calendar events for this week (Oct 26 – Nov 1), maybe workflow runs failing, invites, etc. That''s the honest way to build a to-do list: pull upcoming calendar events this week, unread/actionable mail, maybe pending invites, workflow failures.\n\nI have only one mailbox (ana@harness.test). Unread count appears 0 in first 100 emails, but I haven''t paged the whole inbox. The remaining inbox is probably more edge test emails. But to build a to-do list, I need to find actionable mail — maybe search for things like \"action required\", \"invoice\", \"deadline\", etc. Better: use aggregateInbox (recent 25 per mailbox) — that''s the recent signal. Earlie